{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And they said, saying.</b> The verse says the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> after it already said 'to all the children of Israel.' Perhaps it is because the spies too also said ״אֶרֶץ זָבַת חָלָב״ etc., which is praise of the land, and their intention was to do harm by justifying the statement of the slander, therefore Scripture testifies that Joshua and Caleb intended to exalt and glorify the land, and the word <b>לֵאמֹר</b> here is an expression of exaltment, after the manner of the verse (Deuteronomy 26:17) ״הֶאֱמַרְתָּ וְהֶאֱמִירְךָ״." "<b>The land is very, very good, etc.</b> They intended to refute the words of the spies who said ״אֶרֶץ אוֹכֶלֶת״ etc., and they said ״טוֹבָה מְאֹד מְאֹד״ both in respect of its fruits and in respect of its habitability. And corresponding to the magnitude of the deterrent they said ״אִם חָפֵץ״ etc. ״וּנְתָנָהּ״ in the manner of a gift, not by the force of a man's might, and they concluded by saying ״לַחְמֵנוּ הֵם״."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "Or HaChaim reads lemor (לֵאמֹר, 'saying') through the root אמר/אמר in the sense of Deuteronomy 26:17, where the root conveys declaring greatness or eminence."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:08:45.111772754Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}